  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"When several people request an unclaimed item within its brief decision window, a request sheet records names, request times, and earlier contested-item awards.\", \"decision\": \"How should competing requests for the same item be settled?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nA volunteer draws one name from the competing requests when the item’s decision window closes. Every registered requester has an equal chance for that item, and the result can be announced before it moves to collection.\nA volunteer awards the item to the earliest recorded requester when its decision window closes. The visible request times determine priority, so people can understand the result without a separate comparison of their reasons for wanting it.\nA volunteer awards the item to the requester with the fewest earlier contested-item awards, using request time to break ties. Priority spreads sought-after goods across visitors while retaining a clear rule when their previous awards are equal."
    }
  ],
